  },
  {
    "id": "ghostshield-siloxa-tek",
    "name": "Ghostshield Siloxa-Tek 8500 Penetrating Sealer",
    "category": "Penetrating Sealer (Not a Film Coating)",
    "badge": "Best No-Coating Alternative",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.6,
    "reviewCount": 3200,
    "pros": [
      "Penetrates rather than filming, so it cannot peel, delaminate, or pick up on hot tires",
      "Works on damp or moisture-emitting slabs where film coatings fail",
      "No change to appearance or traction — the floor looks like bare concrete",
      "Dramatically reduces oil, water, and salt absorption into the slab",
      "Minimal prep required and no cure-time restrictions on driving"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Does not hide stains, patches, or surface imperfections — appearance is unchanged",
      "Provides no abrasion layer, so the concrete surface itself still wears"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The right answer for a slab with moisture problems or an owner who does not want the risk of a film coating. Protection without the failure modes.",
    "description": "Every film coating shares the same failure modes: it can delaminate, it can blister from vapor pressure, and it can pick up on hot tires. A penetrating sealer has none of them, because there is no film to fail. Siloxa-Tek 8500 soaks into the concrete and lines the capillary pores with a hydrophobic silane-siloxane barrier, dramatically reducing absorption of water, oil, and de-icing salts. In testing, treated concrete repelled spilled oil long enough to wipe it up cleanly where untreated concrete absorbed it within minutes — which for many garage owners is the actual problem they were trying to solve. Its most valuable application is on slabs that fail the plastic-sheet moisture test. Where vapor migration would blister an epoxy from below, a penetrating sealer is unaffected because moisture can still pass through. Prep is minimal, there is no pot life, and there are no cure restrictions keeping you from driving on it. The honest limitations follow from the mechanism. It does not change appearance, so stains, patch marks, and discoloration all remain visible — if your goal is a floor that looks finished, this is not it. And it adds no abrasion layer, so the concrete surface itself continues to wear normally. As protection rather than transformation, it is excellent."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Concrete Floor Coatings",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-concrete-floor-coating/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best concrete floor coating?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For most garages, Rust-Oleum EpoxyShield Professional is the best DIY choice — genuine two-part epoxy that resists hot tire pickup after full cure. For maximum durability and UV stability, a polyaspartic system like Leggari lasts 10-20 years. For a basement or patio where slip resistance matters more than vehicle durability, a textured one-part coating is the better fit.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long does a concrete floor coating last?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "One-part epoxy paints last 2-4 years in a garage. Two-part epoxy lasts 5-10 years. Polyaspartic and polyurea systems last 10-20 years. Those figures assume proper surface preparation — a coating over an unprepared or oil-contaminated slab can fail within a single season regardless of chemistry.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do I need to grind my concrete before coating it?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Grinding is strongly recommended and is the single biggest factor in whether a coating lasts. Acid etching, which most DIY kits include, fails on hard-troweled floors — which describes most modern garage slabs — leaving almost no profile for the coating to grip. A rented walk-behind grinder runs about $100 a day and is the best money spent on the project.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is hot tire pickup and how do you prevent it?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Tires reach well over 100°F after highway driving, and hot rubber softens weaker coatings, lifting them when the car moves. Prevent it three ways: use a genuine two-part product rather than one-part paint, prepare the surface by grinding, and stay off the floor with a vehicle for the full cure period — usually 5-7 days for epoxy.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is polyaspartic better than epoxy?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For durability and UV stability, yes — polyaspartic stays clear where epoxy ambers, resists abrasion better, cures in hours rather than days, and applies at lower temperatures. The trade-offs are cost and a pot life as short as 20 minutes, which is genuinely difficult for a first-time applicator working alone.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you coat a concrete floor that has moisture problems?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Not with a standard film coating — vapor pressure from below will blister or delaminate it. Test first by taping a two-foot square of plastic to the floor for 24 hours; condensation underneath means moisture is present. Those slabs need a moisture-mitigating primer, a system rated for high vapor transmission, or a penetrating sealer instead of a film.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much does it cost to coat a garage floor?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "DIY with a two-part epoxy kit runs $1-2.50 per square foot, or $400-1,000 for a typical 400 square foot garage. A DIY polyaspartic system runs $2.50-5 per square foot. Professional polyurea and polyaspartic installation runs $5-12 per square foot, or $2,000-4,800 for the same garage.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
      aggregateRating: {
        "@type": "AggregateRating",
        ratingValue: product.rating,
        reviewCount: product.reviewCount,
      },
    })),
