        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="16.9k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#coating-types" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Coating Types</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Our 6 Top Picks</a>
          <a href="#prep" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Surface Prep</a>
          <a href="#application" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Application Tips</a>
          <a href="#hot-tire" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Failure Modes</a>
          <a href="#diy-vs-pro" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. DIY vs Professional</a>
          <a href="#maintenance" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Maintenance</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Almost every concrete floor coating failure traces back to one of two things: the wrong chemistry for the conditions, or inadequate surface preparation. Product choice you can research; preparation you actually have to do. This guide covers both — how the four main coating chemistries genuinely differ, which products we would put on our own floors, and the prep steps that determine whether any of it lasts five years or peels in sheets by next spring.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <!-- SECTION 1: The Four Coating Chemistries, Honestly Compared -->
        <div id="coating-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Four Coating Chemistries, Honestly Compared
          </h2>

          <p>
            Retail labeling in this category is genuinely misleading — products sold as "epoxy" are often one-part latex paint with a small amount of epoxy resin added. Here is what the chemistries actually are.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">One-Part Epoxy Paint</h3>
          <p>
            Water-based acrylic or latex paint with a modest epoxy content, sold in a single can. Easy to apply, cheap, low odor, and forgiving of imperfect prep. It is also the least durable option by a wide margin — expect two to four years in a garage, with hot tire pickup being the usual failure mode. Legitimate for a basement floor or a low-traffic workshop; a poor choice where a car parks.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Two-Part Epoxy</h3>
          <p>
            Resin and hardener mixed on site, curing into a genuinely hard, chemically bonded film. This is the workhorse of the category: strong adhesion, good chemical resistance, and a thickness that levels minor surface imperfections. Its weaknesses are UV sensitivity — epoxy ambers and chalks in direct sunlight — and a relatively long cure that keeps you off the floor for days. High-solids versions are meaningfully more durable than water-based ones.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Polyaspartic</h3>
          <p>
            A subset of polyurea chemistry, engineered for fast cure and UV stability. It stays clear rather than ambering, tolerates cold application temperatures that stop epoxy, and can be walked on within hours. The trade is working time: pot life is often 20-45 minutes, which is genuinely unforgiving for a first-time DIYer, and it is the most expensive chemistry here.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Polyurea</h3>
          <p>
