        <!-- SECTION 3: Surface Preparation: The Part That Decides Everything -->
        <div id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Surface Preparation: The Part That Decides Everything
          </h2>

          <p>
            If a coating peels, the odds are overwhelming that prep was the reason rather than the product. Concrete straight from the trowel is too smooth and too closed for a coating to grip, and any oil, sealer, or curing compound blocks adhesion entirely.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Test for an Existing Sealer</h3>
          <p>
            Pour a cup of water on the floor in several places. If it beads or sits on the surface rather than darkening the concrete within a minute, there is a sealer present and it must be removed before anything will bond. Grinding is the reliable method; chemical strippers work but rarely completely.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Deal With Oil Completely</h3>
          <p>
            Coatings do not bond to oil-contaminated concrete, and oil that soaked in years ago is still down there. Degrease thoroughly, then grind the affected areas — grinding is what actually removes the contaminated surface layer. A visibly clean stain is not the same as clean concrete.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Profile the Surface</h3>
          <p>
            The concrete needs a texture roughly like 120-grit sandpaper for coatings to key into. Two methods work: mechanical grinding with a rented walk-behind grinder and a diamond cup wheel, or acid etching with a muriatic or citric acid solution. Grinding is dramatically better and produces consistent results; etching is cheaper and easier but fails on hard-troweled or sealed floors, which is the most common cause of DIY coating failure.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Test for Moisture</h3>
          <p>
            Tape a two-foot square of plastic sheeting tightly to the floor and leave it 24 hours. Condensation underneath, or a darkened patch of concrete, means moisture is migrating up through the slab, and that vapor pressure will lift most coatings from below. Slabs without a vapor barrier — common in older homes and basements — need a moisture-tolerant system specifically designed for the condition.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 5: Repair, Then Vacuum Thoroughly</h3>
          <p>
            Fill cracks and spalls with an appropriate patching compound and let them cure fully. Then vacuum the floor with a shop vac, twice — grinding dust is fine enough to remain after sweeping and will sit between the coating and the concrete like a release agent.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Grinding Beats Etching, Every Time</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Acid etching is included in most DIY kits because it is cheap and requires no equipment rental. It also fails on hard-troweled garage floors, which describes most modern slabs, producing a floor that looks etched but has almost no profile. A rented grinder is roughly $100 a day and is the single highest-value decision in the entire project.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 4: Application: Getting a Good Result -->
        <div id="application" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Application: Getting a Good Result
          </h2>

          <p>
            Once prep is done, application is straightforward, but the failure modes are unforgiving and mostly about timing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Watch the Temperature and Dew Point</h3>
          <p>
            Most epoxies want 55-85°F, and cure stalls badly below that. More importantly, the slab temperature must be at least 5°F above the dew point during application and initial cure, or moisture condenses on the surface and causes blushing or adhesion failure. Check the forecast for slab temperature, not just air temperature, and remember that concrete lags air temperature by hours.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Respect Pot Life</h3>
          <p>
            Two-part products begin curing the moment they are mixed, and once the pot life is gone, the material is unusable regardless of how much is left in the pail. Epoxy typically gives 30-60 minutes; polyaspartic can be as little as 20. Mix only what you can spread in that window, work in sections, and have everything laid out before you mix the first batch.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cut In, Then Roll, Then Keep Moving</h3>
          <p>
            Cut edges with a brush a section at a time, then roll toward the exit with a 3/8-inch nap roller on an extension pole. Maintain a wet edge and do not go back to touch up an area that has begun to set — it will show. Plan your path so you never coat yourself into a corner.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Broadcast Flakes While Wet</h3>
          <p>
            If you are using decorative flakes, throw them upward and let them fall randomly rather than casting them at the floor, which produces visible clumping patterns. Do it while the base coat is still wet, and do it more sparsely than feels right — you can always add more.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Respect the Cure, Especially Before Driving On It</h3>
          <p>
            Foot traffic is usually fine within 24 hours, but vehicle traffic needs the full cure — often 5 to 7 days for epoxy. Parking early is the most common cause of hot tire pickup, where warm tires soften and lift a not-fully-cured coating. This is the single most preventable failure in the whole project.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Hot Tire Pickup and Other Failure Modes -->
        <div id="hot-tire" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Hot Tire Pickup and Other Failure Modes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Four things go wrong with concrete floor coatings, and each has a specific cause.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hot Tire Pickup</h3>
          <p>
            Tires reach well over 100°F on a highway drive, and hot rubber plasticizes softer coatings, lifting them when the car is moved. It is the classic failure of one-part epoxy paints and of two-part products driven on before full cure. Prevention: use a two-part product, prepare the surface properly, and stay off it with a vehicle for the full cure period.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Delamination From Poor Prep</h3>
          <p>
            The coating comes up in sheets with clean concrete underneath, usually starting at an edge. This means the coating never bonded — sealer, oil, dust, or insufficient profile. There is no repair short of removing and redoing the affected area, which is why prep is worth the extra day.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Blistering and Bubbling From Moisture</h3>
          <p>
            Small bubbles or blisters appearing days after application usually mean vapor is pushing up through the slab. This is why the plastic-sheet moisture test matters before you buy anything. Slabs with active moisture need a moisture-mitigating primer or a system rated for high vapor transmission.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Yellowing and Chalking</h3>
          <p>
            Epoxy ambers under UV, so a garage with a south-facing door that stays open will show discoloration within a year or two. It is cosmetic rather than structural, and the fix is a UV-stable polyaspartic or polyurethane topcoat over the epoxy. In direct-sun applications, skip epoxy as a topcoat entirely.
          </p>
        </div>

