          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Professionals Actually Bring</h3>
          <p>
            Diamond grinding or shot blasting rather than acid etching, which is the largest single quality difference. Fast-cure polyurea and polyaspartic systems with pot lives too short for a first-timer to manage. Moisture testing and mitigation when needed. And a warranty on adhesion, which is the failure a homeowner cannot easily fix.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When DIY Makes Sense</h3>
          <p>
            A two-part epoxy kit on a clean, dry, well-prepared slab is a genuinely achievable weekend project with a good outcome — provided you rent a grinder rather than relying on the acid packet in the box. If the floor has moisture issues, heavy oil contamination, or you want a polyaspartic system, the professional route is worth the money.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: Keeping a Coated Floor Looking New -->
        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Keeping a Coated Floor Looking New
          </h2>

          <p>
            Coated floors are low maintenance, but a few habits meaningfully extend their life.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sweep Grit Regularly</h3>
          <p>
            The main wear mechanism on a coated floor is abrasion from sand and grit ground under tires and shoes. A dust mop or soft broom every week or two does more for longevity than anything else.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Clean Chemical Spills Promptly</h3>
          <p>
            Good two-part coatings resist gasoline, oil, and antifreeze well, but prolonged contact still degrades the finish. Brake fluid is the harshest common garage chemical and should be wiped up immediately.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Manage De-Icing Salt in Winter</h3>
          <p>
            Salt tracked in from winter roads pools under vehicles and is aggressive on both the coating and the concrete at any pinholes. Rinse the floor after heavy salt exposure and consider absorbent parking mats through the winter months.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Use Mats Under Jack Stands and Kickstands</h3>
          <p>
            Point loads from jack stands, motorcycle kickstands, and trailer tongues can dent or puncture any coating. A scrap of plywood or a rubber pad under concentrated loads prevents the most common cosmetic damage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Recoat Before It Wears Through</h3>
          <p>
            Most systems can be recoated by scuff-sanding and applying a fresh topcoat, which is dramatically cheaper than a full redo. Watch the high-traffic path from the door and recoat when the finish there starts to dull, not after it wears down to concrete.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best concrete floor coating?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most garages, Rust-Oleum EpoxyShield Professional is the best DIY choice — genuine two-part epoxy that resists hot tire pickup after full cure. For maximum durability and UV stability, a polyaspartic system like Leggari lasts 10-20 years. For a basement or patio where slip resistance matters more than vehicle durability, a textured one-part coating is the better fit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a concrete floor coating last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                One-part epoxy paints last 2-4 years in a garage. Two-part epoxy lasts 5-10 years. Polyaspartic and polyurea systems last 10-20 years. Those figures assume proper surface preparation — a coating over an unprepared or oil-contaminated slab can fail within a single season regardless of chemistry.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to grind my concrete before coating it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Grinding is strongly recommended and is the single biggest factor in whether a coating lasts. Acid etching, which most DIY kits include, fails on hard-troweled floors — which describes most modern garage slabs — leaving almost no profile for the coating to grip. A rented walk-behind grinder runs about $100 a day and is the best money spent on the project.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is hot tire pickup and how do you prevent it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Tires reach well over 100°F after highway driving, and hot rubber softens weaker coatings, lifting them when the car moves. Prevent it three ways: use a genuine two-part product rather than one-part paint, prepare the surface by grinding, and stay off the floor with a vehicle for the full cure period — usually 5-7 days for epoxy.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is polyaspartic better than epoxy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For durability and UV stability, yes — polyaspartic stays clear where epoxy ambers, resists abrasion better, cures in hours rather than days, and applies at lower temperatures. The trade-offs are cost and a pot life as short as 20 minutes, which is genuinely difficult for a first-time applicator working alone.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you coat a concrete floor that has moisture problems?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not with a standard film coating — vapor pressure from below will blister or delaminate it. Test first by taping a two-foot square of plastic to the floor for 24 hours; condensation underneath means moisture is present. Those slabs need a moisture-mitigating primer, a system rated for high vapor transmission, or a penetrating sealer instead of a film.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost to coat a garage floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                DIY with a two-part epoxy kit runs $1-2.50 per square foot, or $400-1,000 for a typical 400 square foot garage. A DIY polyaspartic system runs $2.50-5 per square foot. Professional polyurea and polyaspartic installation runs $5-12 per square foot, or $2,000-4,800 for the same garage.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Mistakes That Ruin a Concrete Floor Coating
          </h2>

          <p>
            Coating failures are almost always preventable, and almost always decided before the first drop hits the floor.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Acid Etching Instead of Grinding</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Acid etching is included in DIY kits because it is cheap, and it fails on hard-troweled floors — which is most modern garage slabs. The result looks etched but has almost no profile for the coating to key into. Rent a grinder; it is roughly $100 and it is the difference between a five-year floor and a peeling one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Coating Over Oil-Contaminated Concrete</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Coatings do not bond to oil, and oil that soaked in years ago is still in the slab. Degreasing removes what is on the surface; grinding removes the contaminated layer. A visibly clean stain is not clean concrete, and this is the most common cause of localized peeling.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Moisture Test</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Tape a two-foot square of plastic to the floor for 24 hours. Condensation underneath means vapor is migrating up through the slab, and it will blister a film coating from below within months. Slabs without a vapor barrier need a moisture-tolerant system or a penetrating sealer instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Driving on It Before Full Cure</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Foot traffic is usually fine within 24 hours, but a vehicle needs the full cure — often 5-7 days for epoxy. Parking early is the single most preventable cause of hot tire pickup, and it ruins an otherwise perfectly executed floor.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mixing More Than You Can Spread</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Two-part products begin curing when mixed, and once pot life is gone the material is waste regardless of how much remains. Epoxy gives 30-60 minutes; polyaspartic can give 20. Mix in batches sized to a section, and stage every tool before you mix the first one.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

